@h1 What is Wit? h1@

Wit is a prose-first markup language — for the people who write, and for the
systems that read what they write. You type ordinary sentences and they stay
ordinary sentences. Structure, data, and logic appear only where you reach for
them, and nowhere else.

That is the whole idea. Everything below is a consequence of it.

@h2 The problem Wit solves h2@

Markdown is wonderful right up to the moment you need structure. Then it stops.
A captioned figure, a table built from your data, a citation that knows its own
page number, a section that appears only in the internal draft — Markdown has no
words for any of these. The usual advice is to "just drop into HTML," but the
first raw tag shreds the very thing that made Markdown pleasant: the sense that
you were writing, not coding.

The heavier tools swing the other way. HTML gives you total control and takes
away the feeling of writing entirely. LaTeX produces beautiful pages, but asks
for a preamble, a package list, and a compile toolchain before you have written
a word. Each tool is asking you to choose between prose and power.

@h2 The Wit answer h2@

You should not have to choose. In Wit, plain sentences stay plain. Structure is
a first-class citizen — but an opt-in one. A document that is nothing but
paragraphs is already a complete, valid Wit document. When you finally do want a
heading or a table, you add exactly that much structure and no more.

@@@pre
The keeper had not spoken aloud in eleven days.

He had not noticed, until his own voice *surprised* him.
pre@@@

That is real Wit. A blank line starts a new paragraph; @code *surprised* code@
becomes bold. There was no markup to learn before you could begin, and there is
none to clean up after.

@h2 Structure, only where you reach for it h2@

When you do want more, you reach for a @strong node strong@ — a named structure
written @@@code @name … name@ code@@@. Everything around it stays prose.

@@@pre
@h1 Why I like lighthouses h1@

The lamp burned *steady* through the _storm_ — the kind of
weather that, in another life, would have called for company.
pre@@@

Build that and you get clean, semantic HTML: an @@@code <h1> code@@@, a
paragraph, a @@@code <strong> code@@@, and an @@@code <em> code@@@. Build the
very same file to Markdown instead and you get @code # Why I like lighthouses code@
with @code **steady** code@ — one source, whichever output the moment calls for.

@h2 Where Wit fits h2@

@table |rows [[Tool, Reads as prose, Structured nodes, Data + logic, Outputs], [Markdown, Yes, Only via raw HTML, No, HTML], [HTML, No, Yes, No, HTML], [LaTeX, No, Yes, Macros only, PDF], [Wit, Yes, Yes, Yes, HTML · Markdown · PDF]]|

Wit is the row that says yes to all of it — prose that reads like prose, real
structure when you want it, data and logic when the document has to organise
itself, and a single source that compiles to whatever your reader needs.

@h2 The payoff Markdown can't reach h2@

Here is the kind of thing that sends a Markdown author into raw HTML — a figure
with a real caption:

@@@pre
@figure
  @img |src ./lamp.png| |alt The keeper's lamp|
  @figcaption The lamp that never went dark. figcaption@
figure@
pre@@@

That renders to a proper @@@code <figure> code@@@ with a @@@code <figcaption> code@@@ —
semantic, accessible, and still perfectly readable in the source. Tables built
from your data, reusable components, self-assembling bibliographies, and
draft-versus-final sections all work the same way: written in Wit, not smuggled
in as HTML.

@h2 What ships h2@

Wit is a small, honest toolkit — five zero-dependency TypeScript packages (the
parser, the runtime, an HTML renderer, a Markdown renderer, and the command-line
tool) plus a VS Code extension. A clean install pulls in nothing else. You can
use the @code wit code@ command as a writer and never see the packages, or embed
the packages in your own program and never touch the command line.

@h2 What Wit is not h2@

Being honest about the edges is part of the pitch. Wit is a @strong file format strong@,
not a content-management system, not an editor, and not a general templating
engine. It is deliberately @strong not Turing-complete strong@: there are
conditionals and loops for shaping a document from its data, but the single
escape hatch for real computation is a scripting block, used sparingly and on
purpose. And it is young — the syntax is stable enough to write books in, but
the ecosystem is still small. If you need a typesetting engine with fine control
over kerning and page floats, Wit is not that; if you want to write and have
structure when you need it, it is exactly that.

@h2 Who it's for h2@

Two kinds of people. If you @strong write things — reports, essays, theses,
documentation strong@ — and want them in plain text under version control, Wit
keeps your prose clean and your structure real. If you @strong build things strong@
and want a readable source format your program can parse, resolve, and render,
Wit gives you a typed pipeline to hang your tools on. Most people are the first
kind. Both are welcome.

